// Generated by scripts/build-glossary-data.mjs from content-source/glossary. Do not edit by hand.
export const GLOSSARY_SHARD = Object.freeze({"altcoin":{"slug":"altcoin","cat":"basics","en":"Altcoin","contentLocale":"en","term":"Altcoin","aka":["alt","alts","alternative coin"],"short":"An altcoin is any cryptocurrency other than Bitcoin, a broad category that includes Ether, Solana, stablecoins, exchange tokens and memecoins.","body":[["The name comes from \"alternative coin\", because early projects were alternatives to ",["Bitcoin","bitcoin"],". Today the term is used loosely. Some traders exclude ",["stablecoins","stablecoin"]," and only mean volatile coins, while others use \"alts\" for anything that is not BTC or ",["ETH","ethereum"],"."],["Altcoins range from large networks with years of history and deep ",["liquidity","liquidity"]," to tiny tokens launched last week. The smaller the ",["market capitalization","market-capitalization"]," and ",["trading volume","trading-volume"],", the more the price can swing on a single large order, and the higher the risk that a project fails or a team sells its tokens."],"Many altcoins tend to move in the same direction as Bitcoin, often with bigger percentage swings both up and down. Periods when altcoins outperform BTC are popularly called \"altseason\", but they are not regular or predictable. Before buying an altcoin, check its circulating supply, unlock schedule, where it is listed and how much it trades each day."],"example":"Suppose BTC falls 8 percent in a day. A mid-sized altcoin that often moves with about 1.5 times Bitcoin's swings might fall around 12 percent, and a small altcoin could drop 20 percent or more. If you held 1,000 USDT in that small coin, a 20 percent drop leaves 800 USDT, and you would need a 25 percent gain just to get back to 1,000 USDT.","mistakes":["Buying an altcoin only because its price per coin is low, without comparing market capitalization.","Assuming every altcoin will recover after a crash; many never return to earlier highs.","Ignoring liquidity, then finding that selling a large position moves the price sharply against you."],"faq":[{"q":"Is Ethereum an altcoin?","a":"Technically yes, because it is not Bitcoin. In practice many traders treat ETH as its own category and use \"altcoin\" for coins beyond BTC and ETH."},{"q":"Are stablecoins altcoins?","a":"Under the broad definition they are, but most people do not call USDT or USDC altcoins because they are designed not to fluctuate."},{"q":"What is altseason?","a":"Altseason is a slang term for a period when many altcoins rise faster than Bitcoin. It is a description of past market phases, not a scheduled event."}],"title":"What is an altcoin? Meaning and risks explained","description":"Learn what an altcoin is, how altcoins differ from Bitcoin, and why liquidity and market cap matter before you buy.","related":[["bitcoin","Bitcoin (BTC)","Bitcoin (BTC) is the first cryptocurrency, a decentralized digital money with a hard cap of 21 million coins that runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain."],["memecoin","Memecoin","A memecoin is a cryptocurrency inspired by an internet joke, animal or cultural trend, whose price is driven mainly by community attention rather than a product or cash flow."],["market-capitalization","Market capitalization","Market capitalization is the total value of a cryptocurrency's circulating coins, calculated as the current price multiplied by the circulating supply."],["token-listing","Token listing","A token listing is when an exchange adds a coin to its markets, letting users deposit, trade and withdraw it on that platform."],["volatility","Volatility","Volatility is a measure of how much and how quickly an asset's price moves up and down over a given period, and higher volatility means larger, faster swings."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"solana","name":"Solana","symbol":"SOL"},"tool":"/coins","cta":false},"layer-2":{"slug":"layer-2","cat":"basics","en":"Layer 2 (L2)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Layer 2 (L2)","aka":["L2","rollup","layer two"],"short":"A layer 2 (L2) is a network built on top of a layer 1 blockchain that processes transactions off the main chain and posts the results back to it, giving lower fees while borrowing much of the L1's security.","body":[["The most common ",["Ethereum","ethereum"]," L2s are rollups. They bundle hundreds of transactions together and post a compressed summary to Ethereum. Optimistic rollups, such as Arbitrum, Optimism and Base, assume transactions are valid unless someone proves fraud during a challenge window. Zero-knowledge rollups, such as zkSync and Starknet, post cryptographic proofs that the batch is correct."],"Because the cost of using Ethereum is shared across the whole batch, an L2 transaction often costs a small fraction of the same action on mainnet. L2s typically use ETH for gas, and the same 0x address format works across Ethereum and its EVM-compatible L2s.","L2s add their own risks. Some still rely on a single sequencer to order transactions or have admin keys that can upgrade the system. Moving funds back to Ethereum through an optimistic rollup's official bridge can take around a week, although many exchanges support direct deposits and withdrawals on popular L2s."],"example":["Suppose you make 20 small token swaps a month. On Ethereum mainnet each swap costs about 6 ",["USDT","usdt"]," in gas when the network is moderately busy, so 120 USDT a month. On an L2 the same swap might cost about 0.05 USDT, or 1 USDT a month in total. If your average swap is only 100 USDT, mainnet fees would eat 6 percent per trade, while L2 fees would be 0.05 percent."],"mistakes":["Withdrawing to an L2 address from an exchange that does not support that L2, assuming the 0x address makes it the same network.","Having USDC on Arbitrum but no ETH on Arbitrum to pay gas.","Expecting an optimistic rollup's native bridge withdrawal to arrive in minutes."],"faq":[{"q":"Are layer 2 networks safe?","a":"Major rollups inherit much of Ethereum's security, but they also have their own risks, such as centralized sequencers, upgrade keys and bridge bugs. Maturity varies between projects."},{"q":"Do I need ETH on a layer 2?","a":"On most Ethereum L2s, yes. Gas is paid in ETH on that L2, so you need a small ETH balance on the same network."},{"q":"Is Polygon a layer 2?","a":"Polygon's original PoS chain is usually described as a sidechain with its own validators, while Polygon also builds zero-knowledge rollup technology. Check which Polygon network you are actually using."}],"title":"What is a layer 2 (L2)? Rollups explained simply","description":"Learn what a layer 2 is, how rollups cut gas fees, and what to check before moving funds to Arbitrum, Base or zkSync.","related":[["layer-1","Layer 1 (L1)","A layer 1 (L1) is a base blockchain that processes and finalises transactions on its own network with its own consensus and native coin, such as Bitcoin, Ethereum or Solana."],["gas-fee","Gas fee","A gas fee is the payment you make to a blockchain network to process a transaction or run a smart contract, paid in the chain's native coin such as ETH on Ethereum."],["cross-chain-bridge","Cross-chain bridge","A cross-chain bridge is a protocol that moves tokens or messages between two blockchains, usually by locking or burning assets on one chain and releasing or minting matching assets on the other."],["ethereum","Ethereum (ETH)","Ethereum is a programmable blockchain that runs smart contracts, and Ether (ETH) is its native coin used to pay gas fees and to stake for network security."],["blockchain-network","Deposit network (chain selection)","A deposit network is the blockchain you choose to move a coin or token over when you deposit to or withdraw from an exchange, and the sending and receiving sides must use the same one."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"arbitrum","name":"Arbitrum","symbol":"ARB"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"spot-trading":{"slug":"spot-trading","cat":"trading","en":"Spot trading","contentLocale":"en","term":"Spot trading","aka":["spot","spot market","spot trade"],"short":"Spot trading is buying or selling a cryptocurrency for immediate delivery at the current market price, so you actually own the coins once the trade is filled.","body":[["When you buy ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," with ",["USDT","usdt"]," on the spot market, the BTC lands in your exchange account right away. You can hold it, withdraw it to your own wallet or sell it later. The most you can lose is the money you spent, because there is no borrowing involved on a standard spot account."],["This differs from derivatives such as ",["perpetual futures","perpetual-futures"],", where you trade a ",["contract","smart-contract"]," that tracks the price, can use ",["leverage","leverage"],", and can be liquidated. Spot is generally the first step for beginners because it is simpler and has no funding rates or ",["liquidation","liquidation"],"."],"Your costs in spot trading are the trading fee, the bid-ask spread and any slippage when you use market orders. Limit orders that rest on the order book are usually charged the lower maker fee. Exchanges also offer spot margin, which adds borrowing, but that is no longer plain spot risk."],"example":"Suppose ETH trades at 2,400 USDT and you buy 0.5 ETH on spot with a market order. You pay 1,200 USDT plus a 0.1 percent taker fee of 1.20 USDT. If ETH rises to 2,640 USDT, a 10 percent move, your 0.5 ETH is worth 1,320 USDT. Selling with another 0.1 percent fee costs about 1.32 USDT, leaving about 117.48 USDT profit. If ETH fell 50 percent instead, you would still own 0.5 ETH and could not be liquidated.","mistakes":["Confusing the spot market with futures in the app and opening a leveraged position by accident.","Using market orders on thinly traded pairs and losing a lot to slippage.","Forgetting to include both buy and sell fees when calculating profit."],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between spot and futures trading?","a":"In spot you own the coin. In futures you trade a contract on the coin's price, often with leverage, and your position can be liquidated."},{"q":"Can you lose money in spot trading?","a":"Yes, if the price falls below what you paid. But on a standard spot account you cannot lose more than you invested."},{"q":"Can I withdraw coins bought on spot?","a":"Yes. Coins bought on the spot market are yours to hold or withdraw, subject to the exchange's withdrawal rules and network fees."}],"title":"What is spot trading in crypto? Meaning and example","description":"Learn what spot trading is, how it differs from futures and how fees, spreads and slippage affect your result.","related":[["trading-pair","Trading pair","A trading pair is two assets that can be exchanged for each other on a market, such as BTC/USDT, where the first is the base asset you buy or sell and the second is the quote asset used to price it."],["market-order","Market order","A market order is an instruction to buy or sell immediately at the best price currently available in the order book, prioritising speed of execution over a specific price."],["limit-order","Limit order","A limit order is an instruction to buy or sell only at a specific price or better, so a buy limit fills at your price or lower and a sell limit fills at your price or higher."],["perpetual-futures","Perpetual futures (perps)","Perpetual futures (perps) are futures contracts with no expiry date that use a periodic funding rate to keep their price close to the underlying spot price."],["order-book","Order book","An order book is the live list of all open buy orders (bids) and sell orders (asks) for a trading pair, sorted by price, showing how much people are willing to trade at each level."]],"lesson":{"id":"E07","slug":"first-spot-trade","ep":7,"title":"Your first spot trade: from 100 to 0.002 BTC"},"coin":null,"tool":"/compare","cta":true},"candlestick-chart":{"slug":"candlestick-chart","cat":"trading","en":"Candlestick chart","contentLocale":"en","term":"Candlestick chart","aka":["candlesticks","candles","Japanese candlesticks","OHLC chart"],"short":"A candlestick chart is a price chart where each candle shows the open, high, low and close price of an asset over one time period.","body":["Each candle covers a fixed timeframe, such as 1 minute, 1 hour or 1 day. The thick part, called the body, runs from the opening price to the closing price. The thin lines above and below, called wicks or shadows, show the highest and lowest prices reached during that period.","Colour tells you direction. On most platforms a green candle closed higher than it opened, and a red candle closed lower. Some apps let you swap colours, and in some regional settings red means up, so check your chart settings.",["Candles help you see how buyers and sellers fought during each period. A long lower wick means price fell but buyers pushed it back up. A small body with long wicks on both sides shows indecision. Traders combine candles with volume and ",["support and resistance","support-and-resistance"]," levels, but a single candle pattern is not a reliable prediction on its own."]],"example":["Take a 1-hour ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," candle that opens at 64,000 ",["USDT","usdt"],", drops to 63,200, rises to 64,600 and closes at 64,400. The body runs from 64,000 to 64,400 and is green because the close is higher. The upper wick stretches from 64,400 to 64,600 (200 USDT), and the lower wick from 64,000 down to 63,200 (800 USDT). That long lower wick shows sellers pushed price down 800 USDT before buyers took it back."],"mistakes":["Reading a pattern on a 1-minute chart as if it carried the weight of a daily candle.","Acting on a candle before it has closed, when its shape can still change completely.","Assuming green always means up without checking the platform colour settings."],"faq":[{"q":"What do the wicks on a candlestick mean?","a":"Wicks show the highest and lowest prices traded during the candle's timeframe. Long wicks mean price moved far but was pushed back before the close."},{"q":"Which timeframe should I use for candlestick charts?","a":"It depends on how long you plan to hold. Short-term traders use minutes or hours, while longer-term holders usually focus on daily or weekly candles."}],"title":"What is a candlestick chart? How to read crypto candles","description":"Learn how to read a candlestick chart: open, high, low, close, wicks and colours, with a worked crypto example.","related":[["support-and-resistance","Support and resistance","Support and resistance are price zones where an asset has repeatedly stopped falling (support) or stopped rising (resistance), because buying or selling interest tends to cluster there."],["volatility","Volatility","Volatility is a measure of how much and how quickly an asset's price moves up and down over a given period, and higher volatility means larger, faster swings."],["trading-volume","Trading volume","Trading volume is the total amount of an asset traded over a set period, usually shown as the 24-hour volume in coins or in USD value."],["all-time-high","All-time high (ATH)","An all-time high (ATH) is the highest price an asset has ever reached in its trading history, and the opposite, the lowest price ever, is called an all-time low (ATL)."]],"lesson":{"id":"E15","slug":"read-a-candlestick","ep":15,"title":"One candle: what four things does it tell you?"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"last-price":{"slug":"last-price","cat":"derivatives","en":"Last traded price","contentLocale":"en","term":"Last traded price","aka":["last price","LTP","market price"],"short":"The last traded price is the price at which the most recent trade on an order book was executed, and it is the price you usually see on charts and tickers.","body":["Every time a buy and sell order match, the trade price becomes the new last price. Candlestick charts, 24-hour change figures and most price tickers are built from these trades. It tells you what actually just happened on that specific market.",["The last price is not necessarily the price you will get. If you place a ",["market buy","market-order"],", you pay the best ask, and a large order may fill across several levels. The ",["bid-ask spread","bid-ask-spread"]," and ",["order book depth","order-book"]," decide your real fill price, which can differ from the last price, especially on thin markets."],["On futures exchanges, three prices are often shown: last price, ",["mark price","mark-price"]," and ",["index price","index-price"],". The last price can spike briefly on one order book, so liquidations usually use the mark price instead. Stop orders can often be set to trigger on either one, so check your setting."]],"example":["Suppose the last trade on a token was 0.800 ",["USDT","usdt"],", but the order book now shows a best bid of 0.790 and a best ask of 0.812. If you buy 5,000 tokens with a market order, you pay at least 0.812, so your cost is at least 4,060 USDT, not the 4,000 USDT the last price suggests. If only 2,000 tokens sit at 0.812 and the rest at 0.820, your total becomes 1,624 + 2,460 = 4,084 USDT."],"mistakes":["Expecting a market order to fill at the last traded price.","Using the last price to judge liquidation risk on futures, where the mark price applies.","Trusting a last price on an illiquid pair where the most recent trade may be minutes or hours old."],"faq":[{"q":"Is the last price the same as the current price?","a":"It is the most recent trade, which is what most people call the current price. What you can actually buy or sell at is shown by the best ask and best bid."},{"q":"Should my stop-loss trigger on last price or mark price?","a":"Last price reacts faster to real trades, while mark price ignores brief spikes. Choose based on whether you prefer speed or protection from wicks."}],"title":"What is the last traded price? Last vs mark price","description":"Learn what the last traded price means, why your fill price can differ, and how it compares with mark and index price.","related":[["mark-price","Mark price","The mark price is a fair-value price an exchange uses to calculate unrealized profit and loss and to trigger liquidations, instead of the last traded price."],["index-price","Index price","The index price is a reference price for an asset calculated from a weighted average of spot prices on several major exchanges, used to anchor futures pricing."],["order-book","Order book","An order book is the live list of all open buy orders (bids) and sell orders (asks) for a trading pair, sorted by price, showing how much people are willing to trade at each level."],["stop-loss","Stop-loss order","A stop-loss order is an order that automatically closes your position when the price reaches a level you set, limiting how much you can lose on a trade."]],"lesson":{"id":"E13","slug":"price-vs-fill-price","ep":13,"title":"Why isn't the price on screen my fill price?"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"funding-rate-arbitrage":{"slug":"funding-rate-arbitrage","cat":"derivatives","en":"Funding rate arbitrage","contentLocale":"en","term":"Funding rate arbitrage","aka":["funding arbitrage","cash-and-carry","delta-neutral funding"],"short":"Funding rate arbitrage is a market-neutral strategy that collects funding payments by holding opposite positions, usually spot long and perpetual short, so price moves largely cancel out.","body":["When funding is positive, longs pay shorts. If you buy a coin on spot and open a short perpetual of the same size, price moves offset each other and you receive funding each interval. When funding is negative, the reverse setup, short spot via margin and long perp, can collect it, though that is harder to do.","A variation uses two exchanges: short the perp where funding is high and long the perp where it is low or negative. The goal is the same: be neutral on price and earn the difference in funding.","The strategy has real costs and risks. Trading fees on four legs (open and close, spot and perp) can eat days of funding. Funding rates change every interval and can flip negative. The short perp needs margin and can be liquidated in a sharp rally if it is under-collateralised, even though the spot side gained. Exchange risk and withdrawal limits also apply. Settlement intervals differ by exchange and pair."],"example":["Suppose you buy 10,000 ",["USDT","usdt"]," of ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," on spot and short 10,000 USDT of a BTC perpetual with 2,500 USDT of margin. Funding is +0.01% every 8 hours, paying you 1 USDT per interval, or 3 USDT a day. Opening and closing all legs at 0.1% each costs about 40 USDT, so you need around two weeks at that rate just to cover fees. Over 90 days you would collect about 270 USDT, or roughly 230 USDT net, on 12,500 USDT of capital."],"mistakes":["Ignoring trading fees, which can wipe out weeks of funding income.","Using too little margin on the short so a rally liquidates it while the spot side is stuck on another account.","Assuming a high funding rate will stay high."],"faq":[{"q":"Is funding rate arbitrage risk-free?","a":"No. Funding can turn negative, fees add up, the short can be liquidated if under-margined, and exchange risk remains."},{"q":"How much can funding rate arbitrage earn?","a":"It depends entirely on current funding rates, fees and capital efficiency. Returns vary widely over time and are often modest after costs."}],"title":"What is funding rate arbitrage? How it works","description":"Learn how funding rate arbitrage collects perpetual funding with a spot-long, perp-short setup, and the fees and risks involved.","related":[["funding-rate","Funding rate","A funding rate is a periodic payment between long and short traders that keeps a perpetual futures price close to the spot price."],["arbitrage","Arbitrage","Arbitrage is buying an asset where it is cheaper and selling it where it is more expensive at the same time, to profit from a price difference between markets."],["hedging","Hedging","Hedging is opening a position that offsets the risk of another position, so that a loss on one is partly or fully balanced by a gain on the other."],["perpetual-futures","Perpetual futures (perps)","Perpetual futures (perps) are futures contracts with no expiry date that use a periodic funding rate to keep their price close to the underlying spot price."]],"lesson":{"id":"E24","slug":"funding-fees-final","ep":24,"title":"Price flat, balance down? Finish the last level with costs in mind"},"coin":null,"tool":"/funding-rates","cta":false},"hardware-wallet":{"slug":"hardware-wallet","cat":"wallets-security","en":"Hardware wallet","contentLocale":"en","term":"Hardware wallet","aka":["hardware crypto wallet","signing device"],"short":"A hardware wallet is a small physical device that stores your private keys offline and signs transactions inside the device, so the keys never leave it even when it is plugged into a computer or phone.","body":["Well-known examples include Ledger, Trezor and Keystone. You connect the device by USB, Bluetooth or QR code, prepare a transaction in a companion app, and then confirm it with a button press on the device itself. The screen on the device is what you trust, not the screen of your computer.",["When you set it up, the device creates a ",["seed phrase","seed-phrase"],". That phrase is the real backup: if the device breaks or is lost, you can restore the same wallet on a new device. A PIN protects the device against someone who picks it up."],"A hardware wallet blocks remote key theft, but it cannot protect you from approving a bad transaction. Always compare the address and amount on the device screen before you confirm."],"example":["Suppose you want to send 250 ",["USDC","usdc"]," to a friend. You paste the address into the wallet app on your laptop. The hardware wallet screen then shows the address and 250 USDC. You notice the last four characters on the device differ from the ones your friend sent you, because malware replaced the address on your clipboard. You reject the transaction on the device, and nothing leaves your wallet."],"mistakes":["Buying a hardware wallet second-hand or from an unofficial marketplace seller.","Typing the seed phrase into a computer or website to set up or fix the device.","Confirming a transaction without reading the address and amount on the device screen."],"faq":[{"q":"Do I need a hardware wallet?","a":"If you hold an amount you would be upset to lose and plan to keep it for a long time, a hardware wallet is a common way to reduce online risk."},{"q":"What happens if I lose my hardware wallet?","a":"Your coins are not on the device; they are on the blockchain. Restore the seed phrase on a new device and move funds if you think the old device and PIN could be used by someone else."}],"title":"What is a hardware wallet? How it protects crypto","description":"What a hardware wallet does, how on-device signing works, and the mistakes that still put your coins at risk.","related":[["cold-wallet","Cold wallet (cold storage)","A cold wallet is a way of storing crypto where the private keys are generated and kept offline, away from internet-connected devices, so remote attackers cannot reach them."],["seed-phrase","Seed phrase (recovery phrase)","A seed phrase is a list of usually 12 or 24 ordinary words that encodes the master key of a crypto wallet, so anyone who has the words can restore the wallet and spend all of its funds."],["private-key","Private key","A private key is a secret number that proves ownership of a crypto address and is used to sign transactions, so whoever holds it can spend the coins at that address."],["address-poisoning","Address poisoning","Address poisoning is a scam where an attacker sends a tiny or zero-value transfer from an address that looks like one you use, hoping you later copy that fake address from your transaction history."]],"lesson":{"id":"E03","slug":"exchange-vs-wallet","ep":3,"title":"Where are my coins? Exchange vs wallet"},"coin":null,"tool":null,"cta":false},"governance-token":{"slug":"governance-token","cat":"defi","en":"Governance token","contentLocale":"en","term":"Governance token","aka":["voting token"],"short":"A governance token is a crypto token that gives holders the right to vote on a protocol's decisions, such as fee changes, upgrades and treasury spending, in proportion to the tokens they hold or delegate.","body":["Examples include UNI for Uniswap, AAVE for Aave and ARB for Arbitrum. Holders can vote directly or delegate their votes to someone else. Some protocols use vote-escrow models, where locking the token for longer gives more voting power.",["Voting rights do not automatically mean a claim on revenue. Some governance tokens have no direct fee share, so their market value rests on expected future decisions, token demand and speculation. Others have ",["staking","staking"]," or fee-sharing features approved by governance."],["Governance tokens are often distributed through airdrops, ",["liquidity mining","yield-farming"]," and allocations to teams and investors. Check the ",["tokenomics","tokenomics"],": how many tokens exist, when locked allocations unlock, and how concentrated voting power is."]],"example":"Say a protocol has 1 billion governance tokens, with 40 percent held by the team and investors and 60 percent spread among users. You hold 20,000 tokens, or 0.002 percent of the supply. On a vote where 50 million tokens participate, your 20,000 tokens are 0.04 percent of the votes cast. If the team and investors vote together with even 10 percent of the supply, 100 million tokens, they would outweigh every other participant in that vote.","mistakes":["Buying a governance token expecting dividends that the token does not provide.","Ignoring upcoming unlocks of team and investor tokens.","Assuming one token always equals one vote, when delegation and locking can change voting power."],"faq":[{"q":"Do governance tokens pay rewards?","a":"Some do, through staking or fee sharing voted in by governance, but many give only voting rights."},{"q":"Can I vote with tokens held on an exchange?","a":"Usually not. Most on-chain votes need the tokens in your own wallet, or delegated from it."}],"title":"What is a governance token? Voting rights in crypto","description":"What governance tokens are, how token voting and delegation work, and why voting rights are not the same as revenue.","related":[["dao","DAO (decentralized autonomous organization)","A DAO, or decentralized autonomous organization, is a community that makes decisions and manages a shared treasury through token-holder voting and smart contracts instead of a traditional management structure."],["tokenomics","Tokenomics","Tokenomics is the economic design of a cryptocurrency, covering how many tokens exist, who received them, how new tokens are released and what the token is actually used for."],["airdrop","Airdrop","An airdrop is a distribution of free tokens to wallet addresses or exchange users, usually to reward early users, decentralize ownership or promote a new project."],["coin-vs-token","Coin vs token","A coin is the native asset of its own blockchain, such as BTC or ETH, while a token is an asset created on top of an existing blockchain using a smart contract, such as USDT on Ethereum."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"uniswap","name":"Uniswap","symbol":"UNI"},"tool":null,"cta":false},"usdt":{"slug":"usdt","cat":"stablecoins","en":"USDT (Tether)","contentLocale":"en","term":"USDT (Tether)","aka":["Tether","USD₮","tether USDT"],"short":"USDT, also called Tether, is the largest US dollar stablecoin, issued by Tether, designed to stay at 1 dollar and backed by reserves the company reports in regular attestations.","body":[["USDT is the most common quote currency on crypto exchanges and is widely used for ",["P2P trading","p2p-trading"]," and cross-border transfers. It is issued on many blockchains, including Tron (TRC-20), ",["Ethereum","ethereum"]," (ERC-20), BNB Smart Chain, Solana, TON and others. Each network has its own address format and fees, and the receiving side must support the network you send on."],"Tether says USDT is backed by reserves mostly in US treasury bills and cash equivalents, along with other assets, and publishes periodic attestation reports. Over the years, critics have questioned the level of transparency, and reserve composition has changed over time.",["Tether can freeze USDT at specific addresses, often at the request of law enforcement. Availability also depends on regulation: some EU platforms restricted USDT for EU users because it is not authorised under ",["MiCA","mica"],"."]],"example":"Suppose you send 1,500 USDT from an exchange to a friend. On TRC-20 the exchange shows an illustrative fee of 1 USDT and on ERC-20 a fee of 5 USDT. Your friend's wallet supports both, so you choose TRC-20 and paste their address starting with T. They receive 1,499 USDT within a few minutes. Choosing ERC-20 would have cost 4 USDT more for the same transfer.","mistakes":["Sending TRC-20 USDT to an exchange that only accepts ERC-20 deposits for that address.","Assuming one USDT can always be redeemed for one dollar by anyone; direct redemption is for verified customers of Tether.","Ignoring that the cheapest network on one exchange may be expensive or slow on another."],"faq":[{"q":"Is USDT backed by real dollars?","a":"Tether reports reserves mainly in US treasury bills, cash equivalents and other assets, rather than only cash in bank accounts. It publishes attestations, not full audits."},{"q":"Which USDT network should I use?","a":"The one both sender and receiver support, ideally with low fees. TRC-20 and several other chains are often cheaper than ERC-20."},{"q":"What is the difference between USDT and USDC?","a":"Both track the dollar. USDT is issued by Tether and USDC by Circle, with different reserve reporting, regulation and availability across regions."}],"title":"What is USDT (Tether)? How it works and its risks","description":"Learn what USDT is, how Tether backs it, which networks it runs on, and what to check before sending it.","related":[["stablecoin","Stablecoin","A stablecoin is a cryptocurrency designed to keep a steady value, usually 1 US dollar, by being backed by reserves such as cash and treasury bills, by crypto collateral, or by other mechanisms."],["usdc","USDC","USDC is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Circle, designed to stay at 1 dollar and backed by cash and short-term US government securities, with monthly reserve attestations."],["blockchain-network","Deposit network (chain selection)","A deposit network is the blockchain you choose to move a coin or token over when you deposit to or withdraw from an exchange, and the sending and receiving sides must use the same one."],["depeg","Depeg","A depeg is when a stablecoin or pegged token trades meaningfully away from the value it is designed to track, such as a dollar stablecoin falling below 1 USD."],["p2p-trading","P2P (C2C) trading","P2P (C2C) trading is buying or selling crypto directly with another user, paying in local money through bank transfer or payment apps while the platform holds the crypto in escrow."]],"lesson":{"id":"E02","slug":"is-usdt-a-dollar","ep":2,"title":"Is USDT a dollar? Why is it everywhere when you buy crypto?"},"coin":{"slug":"tether","name":"Tether","symbol":"USDT"},"tool":null,"cta":true},"synthetic-dollar":{"slug":"synthetic-dollar","cat":"stablecoins","en":"Synthetic dollar (delta-neutral stablecoin)","contentLocale":"en","term":"Synthetic dollar (delta-neutral stablecoin)","aka":["synthetic dollar","delta-neutral stablecoin"],"short":"A synthetic dollar is a dollar-pegged token backed by a crypto asset plus an equal short futures position, so price moves cancel out and the combined value stays near 1 USD.","body":[["A synthetic dollar does not hold bank deposits. Instead, the protocol holds an asset such as ",["ETH","ethereum"]," or ",["BTC","bitcoin"]," and opens a short ",["perpetual futures","perpetual-futures"]," position of the same size. If ETH rises, the spot holding gains and the short loses about the same amount; if ETH falls, the opposite happens. This is called a delta-neutral hedge. Ethena's USDe is the best-known example."],["The yield usually comes from two sources: ",["staking","staking"]," rewards on the collateral, if any, and funding payments. When funding rates are positive, short positions receive payments from longs, which the protocol can pass on to holders who stake the token."],["The risks are different from a fiat-backed ",["stablecoin","stablecoin"],". Funding can turn negative for long periods, so the hedge costs money. The short positions sit on centralized exchanges, which adds exchange and custody risk. In extreme markets, ",["liquidity","liquidity"]," for unwinding hedges can be thin, which can push the token below its peg."]],"example":"Suppose a protocol receives 1,000,000 USD and buys ETH at 2,500 USD, getting 400 ETH. It then shorts 400 ETH in perpetual futures. If ETH falls to 2,000 USD, the spot ETH loses 200,000 USD but the short gains about 200,000 USD, so backing stays near 1,000,000 USD. If funding pays shorts 0.01% every 8 hours on a 1,000,000 USD position, that is about 100 USD per payment, or roughly 300 USD per day before costs.","mistakes":["Thinking the yield is fixed when it depends heavily on funding rates that can drop or turn negative.","Treating a synthetic dollar as identical to a bank-reserve stablecoin and ignoring exchange custody risk.","Overlooking cooldown or unstaking periods before the staked version can be redeemed."],"faq":[{"q":"Is USDe a stablecoin?","a":"USDe is usually described as a synthetic dollar. It targets 1 USD using crypto collateral hedged with short futures, not cash reserves in a bank."},{"q":"What happens to a synthetic dollar when funding rates go negative?","a":"Short positions then pay longs, so the hedge costs money. Protocols typically use a reserve fund to cover such periods, and yields for holders fall."}],"title":"What is a synthetic dollar? Delta-neutral stablecoins explained","description":"How a synthetic dollar like USDe holds 1 USD with crypto plus short futures, where its yield comes from and what can go wrong.","related":[["funding-rate","Funding rate","A funding rate is a periodic payment between long and short traders that keeps a perpetual futures price close to the spot price."],["hedging","Hedging","Hedging is opening a position that offsets the risk of another position, so that a loss on one is partly or fully balanced by a gain on the other."],["stablecoin","Stablecoin","A stablecoin is a cryptocurrency designed to keep a steady value, usually 1 US dollar, by being backed by reserves such as cash and treasury bills, by crypto collateral, or by other mechanisms."],["depeg","Depeg","A depeg is when a stablecoin or pegged token trades meaningfully away from the value it is designed to track, such as a dollar stablecoin falling below 1 USD."]],"lesson":null,"coin":{"slug":"ethena-usde","name":"Ethena USDe","symbol":"USDE"},"tool":"/funding-rates","cta":false}});
